{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01SqsiajaSsmxv2JdX2Ther7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Only gpu_mem and execution speed metrics. No timing metrics at all. But the `TimingMetricsLayer` is compiled in and the spans ARE being created.\n\nWait, let me look at this more carefully. The `on_close` handler uses `self.span_timings.remove(&id)`. Maybe the DashMap is causing issues. Or maybe `on_new_span` is not being called (so the `span_timings` is always empty when `on_close` fires).\n\nLet me check - the `on_new_span` handler checks the span level:\n\n\n```rust\nif metadata.level() <= &tracing::Level::INFO {\n```\n\nThis means INFO, WARN, ERROR spans are tracked, but DEBUG and TRACE are not. The spans in the code are `info_span!` or `#[instrument(level = \"info\")]`, so they should be tracked.\n\nBut wait - the `env_filter` is `EnvFilter::new(format!(\"{level},p3_=warn\"))` where `level` is `Level::INFO`. Does the `EnvFilter` prevent the `TimingMetricsLayer` from seeing the spans? If the filter is applied to the subscriber BEFORE the layer, then the layer won't see filtered-out spans.\n\nLooking at the subscriber setup, the `env_filter` is added first as an inner layer, then the other layers wrap around it. In tracing-subscriber, the order matters for how events propagate—outer layers see events first before they reach inner layers. So if the `env_filter` is inner, the `TimingMetricsLayer` as an outer layer should still see the spans before they get filtered out.\n\nWait, I'm realizing the real issue might be with multithreading. When using `std::thread::scope` to spawn threads, the global subscriber set with `set_global_default` might not be properly propagating the span context across thread boundaries.\n\nThe `TimingMetricsLayer` stores span data in a `DashMap` keyed by span ID, but span IDs could be getting duplicated or lost when spans cross thread boundaries. Let me test whether `on_n